\title{MO-2: the K rule on a major premise typed through a let variable, decided by the machine (finding E1-F6 without the reference engine)}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{soundness}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-26}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Package "MO-2", 2026-11-25 (21:17 UTC to the first hours of 2026-11-26 UTC). The maintainer's instruction of that day: the reference engine is a fallback for development and shall not decide anything in production, so the machine must decide by itself every declaration it hands to the reference engine today. This package owns the largest class of such declarations: the machine declined a unit that reached the K rule after it had reduced the application of a let variable away (finding E1-F6 of [the working notes of package M5](rep-machine-M5), closed on 2026-11-24 by declining instead of by deciding), and the reference engine decided it. This report says what the official kernel does at that point, why a machine on values could not follow, the change that makes it follow, the hostile exports that were written against the change before it was trusted, what "exactly as the official kernel" rests on and what it does not cover. The patch is not applied and not published.}

\p{\strong{After the review} (2026-11-26): the candidate of sections 0 to 12, the exact-let mode, did not survive two adversarial reviews and is not applied. Section 13 says what fell, and reports a second candidate with a different goal, the K-union rule. The verdict of section 0.1 is superseded by section 13.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ul{
\li{\strong{What changed.} A run that reaches a K comparison whose answer the machine cannot tell from the official one ends without a verdict as before, but the machine no longer declines the unit: its facade checks the unit again in a new \em{exact-let mode}, in a session of its own and without any staging switch. In that mode the application of a let variable is kept as a value of its own (as the official term keeps it) and typed through the type of the variable, and the K comparison is made in every scope state the official kernel can be in with respect to the let variables the type of the major premise goes through. Where all states agree, that is the official answer. Where they differ, the machine knows the state from a scope stack and takes its answer, and it ends the run without a verdict at the places where what it has would stop being the official term or the official state.}
\li{\strong{What the official kernel does, in one sentence.} It types the raw major premise of the K rule; while it is inside \code{infer_let} a let variable is a free variable with its \em{declared} type, and once \code{infer_let} has returned the variable is gone, because the type of a let term is itself a let term and \code{whnf_core} substitutes the \em{value term}. The two types are convertible, the official comparison is not transitive, and a machine has one value for both states. The second half was not known in the project (finding MO2-F1, section 6).}
\li{\strong{On the corpora.} The base declined 86 units for this rule on the eight corpora (Std 1, cedar 1, con-leche 1, navier-stokes-euler 16, cslib 7, Mathlib 60; each tried twice). The final build decides \strong{80} of them, all accepts, each equal to the reference engine's verdict. \strong{Six stay declines of the machine} (one of cslib, five of Mathlib): they apply a let variable by the over-application of a redex, where the mode has no term to keep and gives no verdict (finding MO2-F5). Seven more units run in the mode because the base decided them by a reading that is the official one only inside the scope; all seven are accepted. In 13 K comparisons of the mode the type of the major premise goes through a let variable with two types; the scope states agree in all 13.}
\li{\strong{Hostile exports.} 67 hand-made exports, every verdict taken from the official checker (leanchecker 4.36.0 in Docker): 36 accept, 31 reject. A plain run of the published executable gives the official verdict on 54, accepts 8 that the official checker rejects (derivable statements) and \strong{rejects 5 that it accepts}; a rejection of the machine is final since 22:44 UTC ([the decision](dec-PUU0), [the report of package MO-1](rep-MO-1)). A plain run of the final build gives the official verdict on all 67; its machine alone gives it on 63 and declines 4 by design.}
\li{\strong{"Exactly as the official kernel, on every input" is not reached, and this report does not claim it.} Three things stand against it. (1) The argument of section 7 was wrong twice while it was being built: the second and the third build of this package gave confident wrong verdicts where the base declined or was right (findings MO2-F2 and MO2-F4), each found by an export written for a step the argument did not have, each before anything was handed over. (2) Two classes next to this one are wrong rejects of the published default and of the final build alike, and are not repaired: a major premise that is a beta-redex (MO2-F3, three exports) and one that is a let term (MO2-F6, one export); the same repair would serve both and is described, not built. (3) The six real units above.}
\li{\strong{No accept of a statement that is not derivable} was seen in the base or in any build of this package, on any row, corpus, mutant or test.}
\li{\strong{Gates} (section 8): the suites of the module; the hostile suite under 56 switch sets with the 67 rows; the gate of the brief through the executable and the jars; the machine's own gate of the default set with no difference in any lane; the differential engine on the eight corpora with no disagreement, also with every declaration in the mode; every hostile row with every declaration in the mode. With the old rule switched back on the build computes what the base computes, to the step, on all eight corpora. \strong{Guard} (section 9): passed on the executable (geometric mean -0.1 \%, largest loss +0.6 \%); on the JVM launcher the geometric mean is lower too, but Std is +3.5 \% and +3.3 \% in two windows, above the limit of 3 \%.}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.1 Verdict}

\p{\strong{Go for an adversarial pass by someone who did not write it; no-go for publication before that pass.} For: against the published default the final build takes 80 of 86 units of the class away from the reference engine, repairs five wrong rejects of the default configuration on hostile exports, adds none that is known, passes every gate of section 8, and passes the guard on the executable (no corpus more than 0.6 \% slower; section 9). Against: on the JVM launcher one corpus, Std, is about 3.4 \% slower in two windows, where the guard allows 3 \%; the change is about 1,600 lines in the judgments of the machine that the K rule goes through; its own author found two wrong verdicts in it after its gates had passed, with exports written the same evening; the rule that repaired the last of them is conservative and costs six real units; and the standing policy asks for an adversarial pass judged by the official checker before anything enters the default. Section 11 says where to attack. If the maintainer's aim is that the reference engine decides nothing in production, this patch alone does not reach it: the six units and the units of eager reduction remain.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. The class: what was declined, and where}

\p{The published build of 2026-11-25 20:00 UTC (jars \code{f5517aa135d2}), the executable, 8 workers, each corpus once with the machine's decline log. The published build of 22:45 UTC (jars \code{f88e4ef5e102}, the base of everything from section 5 on) has the same machine jar, \code{1eaaf3e3655c}: only the driver and the wiring changed.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{Decline lines}\th{of them this rule}\th{Units of this rule}\th{Other declines (eager reduction, another package)}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{2}\td{2}\td{1}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{2}\td{2}\td{1}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{4}\td{2}\td{1}\td{1 unit}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{32}\td{32}\td{16}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{22}\td{14}\td{7}\td{4 units}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{128}\td{120}\td{60}\td{4 units}}
}

\p{Every unit is tried twice (the driver's cold retry of a declined unit), which is why the lines are twice the units. The units: Std and cedar \code{Std.IterM.Equiv.lift_step_bind_congr}; con-leche \code{ConLeche.Model.harvestDefn}; navier-stokes-euler 16, among them \code{EulerSmoothTensorLimit.exists_smooth_limit}, \code{NavierStokes.UniformAngularReset.uniform_reset_branch}, \code{FormalMultilinearSeries.comp_assoc}, \code{Memℓp.add}; cslib 7, among them \code{Cslib.Automata.NA.Buchi.buchiFamily_saturation}, \code{Matrix.exists_rank_normal_form}; Mathlib 60, among them \code{AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isCoverDense_toOver_Spec}, \code{AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isOneHypercoverDense_toOver_Spec}, the private lemma \code{aux_basicOpen_of_aux_restrict} of \code{AlgebraicGeometry.QuasicoherentTilde}, \code{GromovHausdorff.instCompleteSpaceGHSpace}, seven private declarations and thirteen of \code{CategoryTheory}.}

\p{\strong{One decline site.} All of them end in \code{\verb~|Reduce.k_iota3~}, the step of the K rule that compares the type of the major premise with the type of the constructor application, with the declaration-wide flag \code{zetaInexact} set. The flag is set in two places and nowhere else, the two places where a let variable of INFER is applied and reduced away: \code{\verb~|Eval.k_evalHead~} (EVAL of an application whose head evaluates to the variable) and \code{\verb~|Eval.apply~}. The rule of 2026-11-24 was: after such an application every K comparison of the declaration declines.}

\p{\strong{Two shapes}, told apart by the counters of the candidate (section 7):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The let variable and the K rule have nothing to do with each other} (80 of the 93 units that run in exact-let mode): a let variable is applied somewhere, and later a K comparison runs on a major premise whose type goes through no let variable with two types. The flag is declaration-wide, so the unit was declined. Minimal exports: \code{mo2-shape-unrelated-ok} and \code{mo2-shape-unrelated-bad}.}
\li{\strong{The type of the major premise goes through a let variable with two types} (13 K comparisons in 4 units: \code{Cslib.Automata.NA.Buchi.buchiFamily_saturation}, \code{exists_seq_forall_proj_of_forall_finite}, \code{GromovHausdorff.instCompleteSpaceGHSpace} and one instance of \code{CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.CoGrothendieck}): the variable itself, an application of it, a projection. Minimal exports: \code{e1-sys-let-krule-major-applied-ok} of package E1 and the rows \code{mo2-app-*}, \code{mo2-proj-*}, \code{mo2-var-*} of section 5.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. What the official kernel does}

\p{The source read is the official kernel as checked out under \code{refs/} (commit \code{611c117bfaf7} of 2026-11-16), files \code{type_checker.cpp}, \code{inductive.h}, \code{local_ctx.cpp} and \code{instantiate.cpp}. The reference engine (\src{rlvm-ref/README.md}) is a port of the same algorithm and agrees with it on every export of section 5.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The K rule types the raw major premise.} \code{\verb~|to_cnstr_when_K~} starts with \code{\verb~|expr app_type = whnf(infer_type(e));~}, where \code{e} is the major premise as it stands in the recursor application, before any reduction of it. If the head of that type is the major inductive, the constructor application is built from the parameters of the type, and the rule fires iff \code{\verb~|is_def_eq(app_type, new_type)~}. Only then is the major premise itself reduced.}
\li{\strong{Substitution does not reduce.} \code{instantiate} replaces bound variables and nothing else. An argument \code{\verb~|x a~} stays \code{\verb~|x a~}; a lambda that lands in a head position inside an argument leaves a beta-redex.}
\li{\strong{Inside \code{infer_let} a let variable is a free variable with a type and a value.} \code{\verb~|infer_let~} checks the type and the value of a binder, compares the type of the value with the declared type, and then extends the local context with \code{\verb~|mk_local_decl(.., type, val)~}. \code{\verb~|infer_fvar~} answers the \em{declared} type. \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} replaces such a variable by its value when it meets it in a head position (\code{\verb~|whnf_fvar~}). So for an argument \code{\verb~|x a~} the infer-only \code{\verb~|infer_app~} starts from the declared type of \code{x} and instantiates its codomain with \code{a}: the arguments are not typed.}
\li{\strong{After \code{infer_let} the variable does not exist.} \code{\verb~|infer_let~} returns \code{\verb~|m_lctx.mk_pi(fvars, r, true)~}, and \code{\verb~|mk_binding~} turns a let variable that the body type \code{r} mentions into \code{\verb~|mk_let(name, type, value, r)~}: the type of a let term is the let term \code{\verb~|let x : A := v; R~}. When that type is compared or reduced, \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} takes its \code{Let} case: \code{\verb~|instantiate(let_body(e), let_value(e))~}. Every occurrence of the variable in \code{R} is now the \em{value term}: \code{\verb~|x a~} has become \code{\verb~|v a~}, a beta-redex if \code{v} is a lambda, and its type is inferred from \code{v}, not from \code{A}. The official message for \code{mo2-escape-var-bad} shows the type as the kernel has it: \code{\verb~|'S.bad' has type let x := S.ax; S.P (Eq.rec 7 x) but it is expected to have type S.P 7~}.}
\li{\strong{The two types are only convertible.} \code{\verb~|infer_let~} demands \code{\verb~|is_def_eq(val_type, type)~}, and the official relation is not transitive (package S's triple: with \code{\verb~|d (x : True /\ True) : Nat := And.rec (fun _ _ => 5) x~} and an axiom \code{h}, \code{\verb~|d intro~} is convertible with \code{5} and with \code{\verb~|d h~}, and \code{\verb~|d h~} is not convertible with \code{5}). So a K comparison can fire under one of the two types and not under the other, in either direction.}
}

\p{For the K rule this gives a state per let variable: the kernel is either inside its scope or past it. Scopes nest, so for the let variables that the type of one major premise goes through, the possible states are a chain: all in scope; the innermost one left; the two innermost left; and so on.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Why the machine could not follow: what its values lack}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The application.} The machine's value of \code{\verb~|x a~} had the variable reduced away. A let variable of INFER is a record of its own (a suspension with the flag LET that carries the declared type, the fix of finding E1-F1), but EVAL of an application forced its head and performed the beta step, and ARGV made an ordinary delayed term that is replaced by its value as soon as that is known. The type of what is left is the type of the reduct. That is the half of the problem that finding E1-F6 named, and the decline rule covered it by a declaration-wide flag.}
\li{\strong{The scope.} The machine never leaves a let: the type that the let rule of INFER returns is the type of the body under the bound values, with the let variable still in it, and that variable keeps its declared type for ever. Inside the scope that is the official reading. After it, the official kernel reads the type of the value. Before the fix of E1-F1 the machine had it the other way round. Neither is right in both states, and the expected-type rule of the default set moves the comparison of a let body with its expected type from after the let to inside it. This half was not known: section 6.}
\li{\strong{One memo for two states.} The machine's memo words and its pair table are keyed by values. A K comparison answered inside the scope is found again after the scope, where the official kernel computes on another term.}
}

\p{The order of weak head normalisation and the K rule is not the problem: the machine types the major premise before it reduces it, as the official kernel does (\code{\verb~|Reduce.iota~}).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The design: the exact-let mode}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 A second run, not another kind of let}

\p{The first run of a declaration is what it was, to the tick. It ends without a verdict, as it did, when it reaches a K comparison that needs the mode (4.5). The facade then resets the machine, runs the same unit again in exact-let mode, and forgets the session after it: nothing the mode builds is seen by another declaration, and nothing another declaration or the first run built is seen by the mode. So no \code{let} of any other declaration pays anything, which is what erratum E1 of [the reconciliation](design-reconciliation-document) asks for: making every let value a variable steered into non-termination, and what a let suspension costs is measured in the notes of package M2.}

\p{The second run has \em{no staging switch}: it is the generic tier, which the gate of package M8 compares with every switch set declaration by declaration. Three reasons, each a part of the argument of section 7: without code blocks EVAL and ARGV are the only places that build the value of an application; without speculative segments no judgment is cut, so a scope stack is exact; and the generic tier has none of the proof-comparison shortcuts whose answers are of the tolerated kind.}

\p{Alternatives that were weighed. \em{Keeping the application of a let variable in every declaration} would change values, memo hits and tick counts in every unit that applies a let variable, and it does not solve the scope half. \em{Typing by the value only} (the machine before 2026-11-24) is the official reading after the scope and wrong inside it. \em{Firing the K rule under any reading} is sound (section 7.1) and would need no second run for the units whose first reading fires, but it accepts what the official checker rejects: the task was the official verdict.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 The kept application}

\p{In exact-let mode an application whose head evaluates to a let variable of INFER, or to another kept application, is a record of its own: a suspension with the flags LET and OPEN over the application node and the pruned frame (\code{\verb~|Values.mkLetApp~}; another record than the tabling record of the same node and frame). It behaves as a let variable does: it is never replaced by its value, weak head normalisation forces it (zeta and beta, the official \code{whnf_core}), it has a free variable in the official sense, and two of them are the same expression only if they are one record. It is made in the two places where the mode's EVAL meets such an application, \code{\verb~|Eval.k_evalHead~} and \code{\verb~|Eval.argvFast~}; forcing it evaluates the application with the record itself as the tabling record, which is how the step tells the forcing from another meeting.}

\p{Its type is the type of its head \em{value}, instantiated argument by argument with the values ARGV gives the argument terms (\code{ENPI}, \code{INST}): the infer-only \code{infer_app}. For a let variable at the head that starts from the declared type. The head is read as a value, not as a term under the frame, because a frame slot can hold a typed binding (the fix of E1-F2), which answers INFER with a lambda's domain and stands for its value everywhere else.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 The K comparison in every state}

\p{The \em{type chain} of a major premise: the let variable at its type root (the major premise is that variable, a kept application whose head is, a projection of one of them, an application of such a projection, a delayed projection term), then the let variable at the type root of that variable's value term, and so on. The root of a value term is read off the \em{term}: the walk over application heads and projection structs goes on only while it meets no redex and no let term, because the value of such a term has the beta or zeta step performed and the official term has not (finding MO2-F4, section 6). A value term that is a redex whose body is typed through its own binders ends the chain: its official type is the binder's domain in every state. Any other redex or let term ends the chain too, and the let variables with two types that it can read through its frame are treated as those behind a lambda body, below. State \code{i} of the chain: the first \code{i} variables have left their scope. The type of the major premise in state \code{i} is computed with those variables read as their value terms (INFER of the value term in infer-only mode, recursively), every other variable at its declared type, and nothing is stored (\code{\verb~|Infer.outType~}). A variable whose declared type \em{is} the type INFER gave its value (one record) adds no state of its own.}

\p{\code{\verb~|Infer.letKSlow~} is called where the standard comparison has answered (state 0). It computes the answer of every further state: the weak head normal form of the type, the constructor application from its parameters, the comparison. Then:}

\ul{
\li{all answers agree: that is the official answer in whatever state the kernel is, and what gets memoised holds in every state;}
\li{they differ: the official state is the number of chain variables that are not on the scope stack (4.4), and its answer is taken. Every variable whose leaving or entering the scope changes the answer is marked.}
}

\p{\strong{Through the body of a lambda.} If a chain variable is bound to a lambda, the type of its application goes on through the type of the lambda's body, and that can go through another let variable. The states above read that variable by its declared type. So the let variables with two types that the body's type can reach are collected (the frame variable at the type root of the body, read off the term; every variable of the frame if that cannot be read off), and the same is done for a major premise that is a delayed beta-redex. While such a variable is in scope the states above are right; it is marked like a variable whose state changes the answer; if it is outside its scope the run ends without a verdict. This rule exists because of finding MO2-F2 (section 6).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.4 The scope stack and the places where a run ends without a verdict}

\p{The let rule of INFER pushes the variable when it binds it (after the type and the value were checked, as \code{infer_let} extends the local context) and the end of the let chain pops what the chain pushed (as \code{infer_let} restores the context when it returns). Without speculative segments nothing else can leave a let, so the stack is the official local context as far as let variables go.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A marked variable leaves its scope} and the type the let chain returns can still hold it (a bounded search of the value; a value without the flag OPEN holds no variable): what was computed belongs to the state inside. No verdict. If the returned type does not hold the variable, nothing outside the scope can meet it again, and the run goes on.}
\li{\strong{A variable marked outside its scope is bound again}: the same, the other way round.}
\li{\strong{The run applied a let variable where no application term can be kept} (finding MO2-F5, section 6): a redex whose body returns the variable is over-applied, \code{\verb~|(fun z => x) a b~}, or the head of the application is a let term whose body returns it. The official term keeps the redex and types it through the body of the lambda, the declared type of the variable; the machine has reduced it away. From then on the next K comparison of the run gives no verdict (the rule of 2026-11-24, inside the mode and for this shape only). A major premise whose type INFER stored while it checked the term is answered before that, from the stored type.}
}

\p{All of them are declines of the machine, together with the lambda-body rule of 4.3. On the eight corpora the first two and the lambda-body rule never occur; the third ends \strong{six real units} (one of cslib, five of Mathlib; section 8), which therefore stay declines. Four hostile rows end in one of these places (section 5).}

\p{The third rule is coarser than it has to be. Where the over-application is part of a weak head normalisation (a kept application is forced, or the redex is the body of a beta step) the official kernel performs the same steps and nothing is lost; the step that applies cannot tell, and a variant that told the forcing of a kept application apart decided none of the six units, so it was taken out again. The exact treatment is to keep that application too: the official term at that point is the application of the let variable to the remaining arguments, a term the machine does not have and would have to build. Not built (section 12).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.5 What starts the mode}

\ul{
\li{The flag of 2026-11-24: the run reduced the application of a let variable with two types away, and reaches a K comparison whose type has the major inductive at its head. (86 units on the corpora.)}
\li{The same for a let variable with one type whose value is a lambda: it can stand for the lambda of another let variable, and the beta step loses the declared type all the same (row \code{mo2-app-alias-exact-bad}).}
\li{The same where the variable is applied by the over-application of a redex whose body returned it (\code{\verb~|Eval.applyFrom~}): the flag of 2026-11-24 was not set there, and the published build answers such a unit by the type of the reduct (finding MO2-F5).}
\li{A mark on the let variable: a later declaration of the session that binds the same variable (the same value term, frame and declared type) may find the value of the application in a tabling record and never apply the variable (row \code{mo2-warm-applied-then-krule-bad}).}
\li{The major premise of the comparison is itself typed through a let variable with two types, as far as that can be read without a judgment (\code{\verb~|Infer.letRootedSlow~}): the variable, a projection, a delayed application or redex whose stored type may have been read through one. The base answered these by the declared type (finding MO2-F1).}
}

\p{The last four are wider than the rule of 2026-11-24. Together they add 7 units on the eight corpora (navier-stokes-euler 1, cslib 1, Mathlib 5), all accepted by both readings; the test in the loop of APPLY adds none of them (with the old rule switched back on, the final build declines exactly what the base declines, section 8.5).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Hostile exports, written before the change was trusted}

\p{67 exports, generator \src{rlvm-machine/src/test/resources/s/gen/batchMO2.py} (package S's builder), rows \code{mo2-*} at the end of the manifest of the hostile suite. Every official verdict is from leanchecker 4.36.0 in the project's Docker image, taken exactly as the suite's other rows were: 36 accept, 31 reject; all 67 came out as predicted from section 2. Fifty-six were written before the first gate of the change; eleven were added late, against the third build, and found the findings MO2-F4 and MO2-F5 of section 6. Every export uses the triple of section 2. With \code{\verb~|R at idx major = @Eq.rec Nat at (fun b _ => Nat) 7 idx major~} the statement \code{\verb~|(fun (_ : R = 7) => True.intro) (Eq.refl 7) : True~} is well typed iff the recursor application reduces to 7: by the K rule, or because the major premise reduces to a constructor. For a rule that must fire to be official the roles are swapped (\code{*-declared-fires-ok}): a let variable declared at \code{\verb~|d intro = d intro~} whose value is an axiom of \code{\verb~|5 = d h~}.}

\p{"Machine alone" is the executable with \code{\verb~|-X no-fallback -X no-recheck~}: the reference engine neither decides a decline nor re-checks a rejection. "Plain run" is the executable without any option, which since 22:44 UTC means: a rejection of the machine is final, a decline goes to the reference engine. Published build = jars \code{f88e4ef5e102}; candidate = the final build of this package, the main tree of 22:44 UTC with the patch (jars \code{afe3ed7fc83d}; section 8 says which jars differ from the published ones). A verdict that is not the official one is in bold.}

