\p{What the table shows. \strong{The published build, machine alone}: 38 declines (the rule of 2026-11-24), 16 official verdicts, 8 accepts of statements the official checker rejects, all of them derivable (kind T; certificates \code{mo2-cert-escape-var-ok}, \code{mo2-cert-escape-app-ok}, and \code{True}), and \strong{5 rejections of exports the official checker accepts} (findings MO2-F1 and MO2-F5). \strong{The published build, plain run}: the reference engine decides the 38 declines, so 54 official verdicts; the 8 accepts and the 5 rejections stay, because nothing re-checks them. \strong{The candidate, machine alone}: 63 official verdicts and 4 declines, each of them one of the places of 4.4 or the rule of 4.3 for a lambda body; the four rows carry the note \code{let-scope} in the manifest. \strong{The candidate, plain run}: the official verdict on all 67.}

\p{The dimensions the task named, and the rows: K types (\code{Eq}; a K type of the export itself with an index, \code{mo2-userk-*}; a universe-polymorphic one at level 2 on types, \code{mo2-upoly-*}; one without indices and with a parameter, \code{mo2-noindex-app-ok}); the major premise a let variable (\code{mo2-var-*}, \code{mo2-escape-var-*}), an application of one (\code{mo2-app-*}), a projection (\code{mo2-proj-*}, \code{mo2-app-of-proj-*}), under a binder (\code{mo2-app-under-binder-*}, \code{mo2-app-bound-argument-bad}, \code{mo2-escape-under-binder-ok}); values that are a lambda, an axiom, a constructor application (\code{mo2-app-neutral-value-*}, \code{mo2-app-ctor-value-ok}); the declared type depending on an earlier let, and being the application of one (\code{mo2-app-dep-let-type-*}, \code{mo2-let-type-function-*}); through a definition that applies its argument (\code{mo2-app-through-defn-*}); two declarations with one let (\code{mo2-warm-applied-then-krule-bad}); a redex between a let variable and its type root, as a value term, as the major premise and as the binder of the value (\code{mo2-chain-redex-*}, \code{mo2-redex-value-escape-ok}, \code{mo2-overapp-*}, \code{mo2-binder-left-let-*}).}

\p{In the suite. \code{SHostileSlowTest} with the rows added, 56 switch sets by 1,341 rows: section 8. The new fast test \code{SHostileLetTest} demands the official verdict \em{of the machine itself} on the 63 rows and on the seven rows of package E1 on the same rule under the generic tier, step 1, the set of wave 6 and the default set, a decline on the four rows with the note, and a decline of the row of finding E1-F6 when the old rule is switched back on.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Six findings}

\subtree{
\title{MO2-F1: after a let, the machine of the published build types by the declared type (a wrong reject of the machine alone)}

\p{Reported on the board at 22:09 UTC, and again at 23:14 UTC when the published default had changed. Export \code{mo2-escape-var-ok}: with \code{\verb~|W : forall (q : d h = 5), P (Eq.rec 7 q)~} and \code{\verb~|r0 : d intro = 5~}, the theorem \code{\verb~|P 7 := let q : d h = 5 := r0; W q~}. The official checker accepts: the type of the value is \code{\verb~|let q := r0; P (Eq.rec 7 q)~}, against \code{\verb~|P 7~} the value term is substituted, the major premise is \code{r0} of type \code{\verb~|d intro = 5~}, and the K rule fires. The machine of the published build, alone, rejects (\code{DECL_TYPE_MISMATCH}): its let variable has the declared type \code{\verb~|d h = 5~} for ever, and the expected-type rule compares inside the scope. No application of a let variable is involved, so the rule of 2026-11-24 did not decline. A plain run of the build of 20:00 UTC accepted, because the reference engine re-checked every rejection. A plain run of the build of 22:45 UTC, in which a rejection of the machine is final, \strong{rejects}: exit status 1 on \code{mo2-escape-var-ok}, \code{mo2-escape-var-alias-ok} and \code{mo2-escape-app-of-proj-ok}, exit status 0 with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}. A fourth export of the family was added late: \code{mo2-binder-left-let-ok}, a let variable whose value is the variable of a redex, with the K rule after the redex has been inferred (the argument is substituted for the binder); the published build rejects it as well. These four are not among the twelve known wrong rejects of [the report of package MO-1](rep-MO-1); no real corpus, published test or mutant is known to be affected (section 8: the candidate's verdicts on all of them are the published ones).}

\p{The same family in the published build: the accepts of derivable statements \code{mo2-escape-var-bad}, \code{mo2-escape-var-alias-bad}, \code{mo2-escape-app-of-proj-bad}, \code{mo2-inside-then-escape-bad}, \code{mo2-escape-inner-only-bad}, \code{mo2-app-alias-exact-bad}, \code{mo2-binder-left-let-bad}. The candidate gives the official verdict on all of them but \code{mo2-inside-then-escape-bad}, which it declines. This is the reverse side of the fix of E1-F1: before it the let variable had the type of its value, which is the official reading after the scope and not inside.}
}

\subtree{
\title{MO2-F2: a confident wrong verdict of this package's own second build, found by its own rows}

\p{Reported on the board at 22:46 UTC, before any gate result of that build was used. With \code{\verb~|let g := fun u => r0; let f := fun u => g u~} (both declared \code{\verb~|Unit -> d h = 5~}) and the K rule on \code{\verb~|f Unit.unit~} after both lets, the type goes on through the body of the lambda that \code{f} is bound to, into \code{g}. The second build compared two states, both of which read \code{g} by its declared type, found them equal and answered: it rejected \code{mo2-escape-lambda-body-let-ok} (official accept) and accepted \code{mo2-escape-lambda-body-let-bad} (official reject, a derivable statement), where the base declines both. The rows were written because the argument for exactness had no line for a lambda body; the official verdicts confirmed the prediction. The final build has the rule of 4.3 for lambda bodies and declines both rows; the same rule covers a major premise that is a delayed beta-redex (\code{mo2-*-redex-body-*}: four rows, four official verdicts). Every gate of section 8 was run again on the final build.}
}

\subtree{
\title{MO2-F3: the K rule on a major premise that is a beta-redex, the reject direction of finding E1-F7 (a wrong reject of the published default, not repaired by this package)}

\p{Reported on the board at 23:56 UTC, as soon as the official verdicts were in. Three exports without any let, written late in the package to test the prediction of 7.3: the statement \code{\verb~|(fun (_ : Eq.rec 7 ((fun (z : d intro = d intro) => z) ax) = 7) => True.intro) (Eq.refl 7) : True~}, the recursor at \code{\verb~|d intro~} with index \code{\verb~|d intro~}, \code{\verb~|ax : 5 = d h~} an axiom; the same with a function (\code{\verb~|(fun (f : Unit -> d intro = d intro) => f Unit.unit) (fun u => ax)~}); the same under a binder. The official checker \strong{accepts} all three: \code{\verb~|to_cnstr_when_K~} types the raw major premise, the infer-only type of the redex is the binder's domain \code{\verb~|d intro = d intro~}, and the K rule fires. A plain run of the published executable (build \code{f88e4ef5e102}) \strong{rejects} all three with \code{APP_TYPE_MISMATCH}, and so does the candidate of this package; with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} both accept. Two controls without the redex (an axiom of \code{\verb~|d intro = d intro~} as the major premise: accept; \code{ax} as the major premise: reject) get the official verdict everywhere.}

\p{The cause is one line: the type of a delayed term without a stored type is the type of its value (\code{\verb~|Infer.vtypeSusp~}: VTYPE of FORCE), so the machine types the reduct \code{ax}, whose type \code{\verb~|5 = d h~} does not let the rule fire. It is finding E1-F7 of [the working notes of package M5](rep-machine-M5), whose accept direction (row \code{e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad}, kind T) is an accepted risk; the reject direction was repaired by the re-check of a rejection until 22:44 UTC and is a wrong reject of the default configuration since. These three are not among the twelve known wrong rejects of [the report of package MO-1](rep-MO-1). No real corpus, published test or mutant is known to be affected.}

\p{\strong{Not repaired here}, and why: the class has no let variable, so nothing of the exact-let mode sees it, and a repair has two parts that both reach into the default tier. (1) At the K rule, a delayed major premise whose type root is the binder of a redex of its own term (or of a let inside it) must be typed by INFER of the term under its frame in infer-only mode, which already gives the official reading (the rows \code{e1-app-type-is-domain-*} are strict rows and pass): a syntactic test and one judgment at a place that is memoised. (2) A delayed argument whose value is already known is replaced by that value when the application is built, so the major premise can arrive as the reduct, with nothing left to say that it was a redex; an exact rule has to keep such an argument as a record of its own, as 4.2 does for the application of a let variable, either in every declaration (new values in the default tier and in the code blocks) or behind a trigger and a second run (the trigger would be the beta step of a redex whose body is typed through its own binder, which is what every \code{\verb~|have h : T := p; h ..~} of a real export is: how many units that would run twice was not measured). The three exports, the two controls, their official verdicts and the generator are handed over with the patch as a package of their own; they are not rows of the suite, because the gate allows the count of known wrong rejects to go down only.}
}

\subtree{
\title{MO2-F4: a wrong reject of this package's own third build, found by its own probes (the chain of let variables went through a beta step)}

\p{Reported on the board at 00:08 UTC of 2026-11-26, and the build was withdrawn. With \code{\verb~|let y : d intro = 5 := r2~} (\code{\verb~|r2 : d h = 5~} an axiom) and \code{\verb~|let x : d intro = d intro := (fun (z : d intro = 5) => z) y~}, the K rule on \code{x} after both lets. Officially the major premise is the redex \code{\verb~|(fun z => z) r2~}, typed through the binder's domain: the rule fires, and the published build, which reads the declared type of \code{x}, accepts by luck. The third build of this package found the successor of \code{x} in the chain of 4.3 by \em{evaluating} the value term of \code{x}: the evaluation performs the beta step and arrives at \code{y}, so the state "both variables have left" read the type of \code{r2}, and the build \strong{rejected} an export that the official checker and the published build accept (rows \code{mo2-chain-redex-inexact-ok} and \code{mo2-chain-redex-fn-inexact-ok}). All gates of that build had passed: no real unit, published test, mutant or earlier row has this shape. The final build reads the root off the term (4.3); the two rows, their mirror and four rows with a let variable of one type are decided with the official verdict. The same evaluation was used for a delayed projection whose struct is a redex; it is guarded the same way.}
}

\subtree{
\title{MO2-F5: the application of a let variable by an over-application was never flagged (a wrong reject of the published default in this package's own class)}

\p{Reported on the board at 00:12 UTC of 2026-11-26. \code{\verb~|let f : Unit -> d intro = d intro := fun u => ax~} and the K rule on \code{\verb~|(fun (z : Unit) => f) Unit.unit Unit.unit~}. Officially the major premise is typed through the body of the lambda, the declared type of \code{f}, and the rule fires. In the machine the beta step returns the variable and the loop of APPLY goes on with it: the test of finding E1-F6 stands at the entry of APPLY and in EVAL of an application, not in the loop. So the published build does not decline: it types the reduct \code{ax} and \strong{rejects} (row \code{mo2-overapp-declared-fires-ok}; the mirror \code{mo2-overapp-value-fires-bad} is accepted, a derivable statement). The third build of this package did the same. The final build sets the flag in the loop, so the first run ends and the unit runs in exact-let mode; there the first row is decided with the official verdict (the type of the major premise that INFER stored is the official one) and the mirror is declined (4.4). The price is paid on real inputs: six units of the class do exactly this, and the mode gives no verdict on them (section 8). The third build accepted them, with the reference engine's agreement, by typing the reduct; that is the reading this finding shows to be wrong on a hostile input.}
}

\subtree{
\title{MO2-F6: a let term as the major premise (a wrong reject of the published default, not repaired by this package)}

\p{Reported on the board at 00:55 UTC of 2026-11-26. \code{\verb~|(fun (_ : Eq.rec 7 (let w : d intro = d intro := ax; w) = 7) => True.intro) (Eq.refl 7)~}: the official checker \strong{accepts} (\code{infer_let} types the body under \code{w} with its declared type, and the K rule fires), the published executable and the candidate \strong{reject}; the mirror with \code{\verb~|let w : d h = 5 := r0; w~} is rejected officially and accepted by both (a derivable statement). The cause is that of MO2-F3: the delayed let term has no stored type, so its type is the type of its value, and EVAL has performed the zeta step. The let variable is one of EVAL, not of INFER: no record carries its declared type when the K rule runs, so neither the flag of finding E1-F6 nor the exact-let mode sees it.}

\p{\strong{One repair for MO2-F3 and MO2-F6}, proposed and not built: at the K rule, a major premise that is still a delayed term without a stored type, and whose term has a redex or a let term on the way to its type root, is typed by INFER of the term under its frame in infer-only mode instead of by its value. That is the official reading for all five exports of the two findings and for the over-application of MO2-F5 inside a scope. It changes the default tier, so it needs the gates of a switch and an adversarial pass of its own; and it does not cover a major premise that arrives as a value because the delayed term had been forced before (7.3).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Exactness: the argument, what it rests on, what it does not cover}

\subtree{
\title{7.1 Soundness does not depend on any of this}

\p{The K rule replaces the major premise by a constructor application of a type that the machine's comparison found convertible with a type of the major premise. Both the declared type of a let variable and the type of its value are types of it (the let rule checked their conversion), the inductive is a proposition with one constructor without fields, so the replacement is definitional proof irrelevance. Firing under any of the readings is derivable; not firing is always derivable. A wrong reading can make the machine accept a derivable statement that the official checker rejects, or reject one it accepts. It cannot make it accept a statement that is not derivable.}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.2 The claim}

\p{For a declaration that the machine decides, and a K comparison on a major premise whose type chain (4.3) has length \code{k}: if \code{k = 0} the machine does what it did. Otherwise the answer the machine uses is the answer of the standard comparison in the state the official kernel is in, where the state is the number of chain variables whose \code{infer_let} has returned; and no answer computed in one state is used in another state in which it differs.}

\p{It rests on:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The value in the major position is the official term}, for these shapes, up to the machine's delayed substitution. A let variable is its record. A syntactic application of one is a kept record, because the mode has no code blocks and the two steps of 4.2 are the only places where its EVAL can meet one; an application that is not syntactic (the loop of APPLY goes on with a let variable that a body returned, or the head term is not a variable) is not kept, and the run gives no verdict at its next K comparison (4.4). Projections of both are projection records; a delayed projection term is typed through its struct if no redex and no let term lies on the way to the struct's root. Checked by the rows of section 5 and by the audits of the gate (memo re-computation with kept applications in the values).}
\li{\strong{The type of a state is the official type of the term of that state.} State 0: the declared type of the root variable, instantiated; arguments are not typed. State \code{i}: the value term of a variable typed by INFER in infer-only mode, which is what \code{infer_type} does on that term. A lambda as a value: the type of the lambda instantiated, which infers the body under the argument with the typed-binding rule. The chain goes from a variable to the next one only through a value term whose root is reached without a redex or a let term, so no state reads a type through a beta or zeta step that the official term does not have (the error of finding MO2-F4).}
\li{\strong{The scope stack is the official local context} for let variables: pushed after the checks of a binder, popped where the chain returns, no cut in between because the mode has no speculative segment; a failed judgment ends the declaration.}
\li{\strong{Everything else is the generic tier}: weak head normal form, the constructor application, the comparison. Its agreement with the official kernel is what the project's gates measure; it is not proven.}
\li{\strong{The mode is reached} whenever a comparison of this class is reached: 4.5.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.3 What it does not cover}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The places where the mode declines} (4.3, 4.4): a hostile input can turn a verdict into a decline there. Never a verdict into another verdict, as far as the argument of 7.2 holds; it held for neither the second nor the third build of this package (findings MO2-F2 and MO2-F4), both times because a step of it had not been written down, and both times a hostile export written for that step found it. With the reference engine off a decline of one unit is a decline of the file.}
\li{\strong{A typed binding on the way}: a let variable whose value is the variable of a redex that INFER is inside (\code{\verb~|(fun (z : D) => let x := z; ..) a~}). While the redex is being inferred the official type of \code{z} is \code{D}; after it, \code{a} has been substituted. The machine has one record for both, as it had for a let variable, and no scope stack for it: state 1 of the chain reads \code{D} (INFER on the term), the later states read the argument (the chain is followed through the value). The two rows \code{mo2-binder-left-let-*} get the official verdict, and so do three probes that are not rows, in which the comparison runs after the redex and inside an outer let variable that the argument is (the published build and the candidate alike). That is five exports, not an argument: the machine cannot tell the two states of a redex binder apart, and a shape in which they differ and the machine picks the wrong one was looked for only as far as these five.}
\li{\strong{A major premise that is a beta-redex, or has one at its type root, when the redex has already been forced.} The machine then types the reduct, the official kernel the redex through its binder's domain. This is finding E1-F7 of [the working notes of package M5](rep-machine-M5), an accepted risk as far as accepts go ([accepted risks](accepted-risks)). It has a reject direction too, and that direction is \em{not} tolerated by the policy once the re-check is off: finding MO2-F3 of section 6, three exports that the official checker accepts and a plain run rejects, in the published build and in the candidate alike. Not this package's class (no let variable); nothing here changes it. A redex that is still delayed, with a stored type, is covered (4.3, 4.5).}
\li{\strong{The other places where the official kernel types an unreduced term}: the struct of a stuck projection in proof irrelevance, structure eta, the unit-like rule, eta expansion. In the default set they see the reduct's type when the application of a let variable was reduced away, and nothing starts the mode for them. Rows of package E1 (\code{e1-sib-proj-*}) are kind T rows for this reason. Inside the mode a kept application gives them the declared type, which is the reading inside the scope; the scope states are compared for the K rule only.}
\li{\strong{A type that an earlier declaration of the session left in a tabling record} and that holds a let variable, used by a later declaration that never binds the variable (an infer-only inference of a shared closed term that an earlier declaration checked). The later declaration is officially outside the scope; the machine types a memoised reduct by its value, which is the official reading there up to the redex class above. The mark of 4.5 covers the declaration that binds the variable again.}
\li{\strong{A frame slot that is a typed binding whose value is a let variable}, as the head of a kept application inside a value term: the state type is computed by INFER, which answers the binding's domain. Not constructed as an export.}
\li{\strong{Eager reduction.} A unit that enters eager reduction in the mode is declined (the generic tier has no \code{eager}). None on the eight corpora.}
\li{\strong{Work, not verdicts.} A kept application has a free variable in the official sense, so in the mode Nat literal folding in lazy delta and the \code{Bool.true} test are skipped where the official kernel skips them; in the first run they are not. This follows erratum E1 and changes no answer.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Gates}

\p{Everything in this section was run on the final build: a private copy of the main tree of 2026-11-25 22:44 UTC with the patch of section 10, built with \src{scripts/build.sh} (jars \code{0397eb07054a}; the jar of \code{rlvm-machine} \code{6db085f6b0a5}; executable 68,487,288 bytes), GraalVM Community Edition, on the aarch64 development host. "Base" is the published build of 22:45 UTC (jars \code{f88e4ef5e102}). The jars of \code{rlvm-driver} and \code{rlvm-main} of the final build are byte for byte the published ones; besides the jar of the machine, those of \code{rlvm-truffle} and \code{rlvm-inductive} differ from the published ones in \em{any} build of that tree (five classes of the Truffle tier and one build file: the main tree is ahead of the published build there, and it is not this patch), which is why the guard of section 9 has a second base. None of the runs of this section is a timing; every one ran behind \src{bench/slot.sh}, off the measurement cores. The gates of the three earlier builds are void and are not reported, with one exception that is named.}

\subtree{
\title{8.1 The suites of the module and the hostile suite}

\ul{
\li{\code{rlvm-machine}, default group, corpora present: 458 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, 4 skipped. Fast group: 427 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, 3 skipped. Three tests are new (\code{SHostileLetTest}); the size test of the stepper convention passes.}
\li{\code{SHostileSlowTest}: 56 switch sets by 1,341 rows (the 1,274 rows of the base and the 67 of section 5), no unexpected verdict. In the base the rows \code{e1-sys-let-krule-major-applied-bad} and \code{e1-sib-krule-major-proj-applied-let-bad} are declines in all 56 lanes and \code{e1-sys-let-krule-major-applied-ok} in 31; with the patch they are verdicts of the machine in every lane, the official ones.}
\li{\strong{Every declaration in exact-let mode} (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letexact=always -X machine.switches=none~}, the machine alone, the JVM launcher) on all 1,341 rows: 1,250 official verdicts; 16 accepts of official rejects, all on rows of kind T, none on a row of this package; 4 rejections of official accepts, all four on rows that are known wrong rejects of every switch set (three rows \code{r2-id-binder-*} and \code{s3-wc-canon-fvar-full-first-ok}); 71 declines: 63 for eager reduction, which the generic tier does not have, the 4 rows with the note \code{let-scope}, 3 rows that the pipeline declines by design and one metadata row. So no row on which the mode gives a verdict that is neither the official one nor a deviation the base has under every switch set.}
}
}

