\title{8.2 The gate of the brief}

\p{\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --dist <the final build> --defaults --corpus all --hostile <the manifest with the 67 rows>~}, through the executable and through the jars.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Launcher}\th{Published tests}\th{Mutants (17,775)}\th{Hostile rows (1,341)}\th{Result}}
\tr{\td{executable}\td{230 of 230 correct, none declined}\td{SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 16,092, declined 1,683}\td{agree 1,251, kind T accepts tolerated 74, known wrong rejects 12 (12 marked, at most 12), wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4, errors 0}\td{PASS}}
\tr{\td{JVM launcher (the jars)}\td{230 of 230 correct, none declined}\td{SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 16,092, declined 1,683}\td{agree 1,251, kind T accepts tolerated 74, known wrong rejects 12 (12 marked, at most 12), wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4, errors 0}\td{PASS}}
}

\p{The differential self-test of the gate caught its planted difference in both runs. The base's own gate ([the report of package MO-1](rep-MO-1)): 230 of 230, the same mutant counts, and on its 1,274 rows agree 1,184, tolerated 74, known wrong rejects 12, declined 4. So all 67 new rows are in agreement in a plain run and nothing else moved; the four declined rows are the base's (none of this package's).}
}

