\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{\strong{1.563 (8 corpora)}}\td{\strong{1.546 against 1.563 (-1.1 \%, 8 corpora); largest loss +3.3 \% (Std), largest gain -5.1 \% (cslib)}}\td{\strong{1.527 against 1.563 (-2.3 \%, 8 corpora); largest loss +1.4 \% (Std), largest gain -8.9 \% (cslib)}}}
}

\p{\strong{Reading.} On this launcher three rounds do not resolve 3 \% on most corpora: the control, which runs the base's machine code, differs from the base by +1.4 \% to -8.9 \% in the same window, and the candidate's cell on navier-stokes-euler is +4.2 \% in the first window and -2.6 \% in the second, on Mathlib -7.5 \% and -0.3 \%. \strong{One cell is the same in both windows and above the limit: Std, +3.5 \% and +3.3 \%} (six rounds of the base 0.861 to 0.911 s, six of the candidate 0.902 to 0.956 s; the control is at +1.4 \%). The geometric mean of the candidate is 0.1 \% and 1.1 \% below the base. So, read strictly, the patch \strong{does not pass the guard on the JVM launcher}: one corpus is about 3.4 \% slower, where 3 \% is allowed. On the executable, where the inlining of the machine's steps is fixed by lists, the same corpus is 0.2 \% faster. The cause on the JVM launcher was not looked for; the first run of a declaration gained one test in ARGV, one in the loop of APPLY and a call in the K comparison, and the bytecode of several hot steps changed size.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. The patch}

\p{Three files, against the main tree of 2026-11-25 22:44 UTC (the tree with the change of package MO-1, published as build \code{f88e4ef5e102}), dry-run verified against it. This is the fourth build of the package; the second and the third were withdrawn after the findings MO2-F2 and MO2-F4.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The mode} (\code{rlvm-machine} only, six files): \code{Values} (the kept application: \code{mkLetApp}, \code{isLetApp}, \code{isLetFlagged}); \code{Eval} (\code{k_evalHead} and \code{apply} call a slow path when the head is a let variable; the loop of \code{applyFrom} tests whether the value a body returned is one; \code{argvFast} keeps the application in the mode; \code{force} evaluates a kept application); \code{Infer} (the mode's state in \code{Infer.Ext}, the scope stack in the let rule, the type of a kept application, the K comparison in every state, the trigger); \code{Reduce} (the three steps of the K rule call the trigger and the comparison); \code{MachineSession} (the second run, the session around it, a measurement aid); one paragraph of \src{rlvm-machine/README.md}.}
\li{\strong{The tests and the rows}: \code{SHostileLetTest} (three tests, fast group), 67 rows and one note in the manifest of the hostile suite, the generator.}
\li{\strong{The 67 exports} (compressed, binary): a tar file with paths from the repository root.}
}

\p{\strong{Not touched}: the eager-reduction code (\code{Conv}, \code{Machine}), which another package works on. In \code{MachineSession.check0} the second run is a block of its own after the eager restart; a merge with a change of that method is mechanical. \strong{No new switch, no new frame, no change of \code{Layout} or \code{Op}}: the kept application is a new value of the field \code{aux} of a suspension, the marks use memo word 1 of a let record (unused but for the mark of 2026-11-24), and the mode's own judgments are run to completion with \code{Machine.drive}, as the audits do. Every new method off the hot path has a name ending in \code{Slow}, so the build's inline lists keep it out of line (94 entries in the never-inline list where the published build has 81, the force-inline list unchanged at 269) and the size test of the stepper convention passes.}

\p{\strong{Beside the patch, not in it}: the five exports of finding MO2-F3 and the two of finding MO2-F6, with their official verdicts and their generator. They are not rows: the four official accepts among them, marked as known wrong rejects, would raise that count from 12 to 16, and the gate lets it go down only (\src{scripts/gate.sh}).}

\p{\strong{Settings} (system properties, read when a session is made, so they work in the executable): \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letexact=off~} declines as before 2026-11-25, \code{=always} runs every declaration in the mode; \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letwide=false~} and \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letroot=off|count~} take the wider triggers of 4.5 out. With all three off the candidate declines what the rule of 2026-11-24 declined and, beyond it, a unit that applies a let variable by an over-application before a K comparison; on the eight corpora there is no such unit that the old rule did not decline already (section 8.5). \strong{Statistics}: \code{machine.let.restarts}, \code{units}, \code{apps}, \code{kDual}, \code{kDisagree}, \code{kFlips}, \code{declines}, \code{lamUnits}, \code{rootUnits}.}
}

\subtree{
\title{11. What an adversarial reviewer who did not write this must still attack}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The lambda-body rule} (4.3): it was added after a finding, it is conservative by a syntactic reading of a term, and its two escape rows are declines. Attack the reading: a body whose type root is behind a redex or a let inside the body, a kept application with more or fewer arguments than the lambda has binders, a lambda reached through two closures, a frame beyond 63 slots.}
\li{\strong{The trigger} (4.5): find a K comparison of this class that the first run answers without starting the mode. Candidates: a major premise that is a delayed term without a stored type whose forcing reduces no let application but whose reduct is typed through a let variable; a value built by a code block; the session path of 7.3. Finding MO2-F5 was of this kind: a third place where a let variable is applied. The places were found by reading \code{Eval}; \code{Code}, \code{Rules} and \code{Elab} were read only for their calls of APPLY.}
\li{\strong{Every evaluation of a term inside the mode's own steps}: \code{letAppWalkSlow} and \code{letAppHead} evaluate the head term of a kept application (a variable by construction), \code{letValueType} and \code{letChainNextSlow} the value term of a chain variable (guarded since MO2-F4), \code{suspEval} the struct of a delayed projection (guarded). Attack the guard \code{plainRoot}: a head or a struct reached through a variable that is bound to a closure or to a delayed redex.}
\li{\strong{The stored type}: a delayed major premise whose type INFER stored is answered from that type, which is the reading inside the scopes it was checked in. The lambda-body rule covers it when the term is not a projection; attack a stored type that is used after a let variable of its frame has left its scope without that rule seeing the variable.}
\li{\strong{The marks and the two declines of 4.4}: a comparison whose answers differ, followed by a use of the memoised answer in the other state that passes neither the end of the let chain nor a new binding of the variable. The argument is that a value holding the variable can only be made from an environment that binds it or from the type the chain returns; attack the bounded search and the word "holds" (a value that reaches the variable only through a memo word).}
\li{\strong{The typed binding as the head of a kept application} (7.3).}
\li{\strong{The redex class} (7.3, findings E1-F7 and MO2-F3): the three exports of MO2-F3 are the simplest shape. Still to write: the redex forced before the K rule meets it (the argument arrives as the reduct), a redex inside a value term of a let variable of the chain of 4.3, a let term as the major premise, and the same shapes at the other typing sites.}
\li{\strong{The other typing sites} (7.3): the same question as MO2-F1 for proof irrelevance on a stuck projection after a let.}
\li{\strong{Budgets}: the mode's own judgments nest to 16 levels and the lambda-body search takes 256 steps; beyond that it declines. A real unit that needs more would decline.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{12. Not measured, not done}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The x86-64 architecture of the arena}: nothing of this package ran there.}
\li{\strong{The six real units} (4.4, 8.4). The exact treatment of an over-application is to keep it: the official term at that point is the application of the let variable to the arguments that are left, which the machine would have to build as a term of its own and keep as it keeps a syntactic application. Not built. A cheaper variant, telling the forcing of a kept application apart, was built, decided none of the six and was taken out.}
\li{\strong{The repair of MO2-F3 and MO2-F6} (section 6): typing a delayed major premise as a term at the K rule. Not built; it changes the default tier. How many real units have such a major premise was not counted.}
\li{\strong{Deciding the two rows of the lambda-body rule} instead of declining them: the state of a let variable behind a lambda body needs the body's value typed in that state, which the chain of 4.3 would do if it went through the beta step with the binder's domain in hand. Not built.}
\li{\strong{Comparing the states in the first run}, without a second run, and repeating the unit only when they differ, would save the seven extra runs; it would run the mode's judgments under speculative segments, which this package avoided.}
\li{\strong{An adversarial pass by another author.} Every hostile export of this package was written by the author of the change. They found two wrong verdicts of the change and three classes of wrong rejects of the published build in one evening, which says more about how many are left than about how many were found.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{13. After the review: the exact-let mode fell, and the K-union rule (second candidate)}

\p{Written in the night from 2026-11-25 to 2026-11-26 (03:35 to about 06:30 UTC of 2026-11-26). Sections 0 to 12 are kept as the record of the first candidate. Their verdict is superseded: two reviews of that candidate ended with no-go, and it is not applied.}

\subtree{
\title{13.1 What fell}

\ul{
\li{\strong{[The review from the source](rep-RV-2A)}: 56 exports with official verdicts. A plain run of the first candidate rejects 17 official accepts, six of them new with the patch: a typed binding whose value is a let variable as the head of a kept application; a struct and a value term whose head variable is bound to a closure (the guard of finding MO2-F4 tested the term, not what its variables are bound to); proof irrelevance on a stuck projection of a kept application (the scope states exist for the K rule only); and two exports that the second run rejects in a part with no let at all, because the second run is the generic tier and the generic tier has wrong rejects that the default set has not. Two binders that are one record defeat the scope stack. Four shapes of the class never start the mode.}
\li{\strong{[The review from the outside](rep-RV-2B)}: 12,621 inputs (generated exports, the 75 real units of the class as small exports, 1,311 structural mutants and 654 hostile grafts of them). The first candidate: 558 wrong rejects of which 142 its own, 422 accepts of official rejects of which 103 its own.}
\li{\strong{No accept of a statement that is not derivable} in either review, in any build.}
}

\p{Both reviews say what sections 6 and 7 had already recorded twice: finding out from a machine on values \em{which} state the official kernel is in (scope stack, marks, chains of let variables, guards on terms) keeps failing, and each failure is a confident wrong reject.}
}

\subtree{
\title{13.2 The K-union rule}

\p{The orchestrator changed the goal of the candidate from "the official verdict by reconstruction" to what the [standing policies](standing-policies) ask in this zone: never accept a statement that is not derivable; accept what the official checker accepts; a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through its incompleteness may be accepted (kind T). Every accept of the machine is a derivation: firing the K rule under any valid type of the major premise is sound, and so is not firing (7.1). So the machine does not have to know the official state; it has to know when its \em{rejection} cannot be trusted.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Variant A.} In the first run, at a K comparison of the class (the flag of finding E1-F6, or a major premise typed through a let variable with two types as far as that can be read without a judgment, 4.5), the run does not end. It goes on with the type the machine has and remembers that the unit is \em{unsure}. If the run accepts, the unit is accepted. If it rejects and the unit is not unsure, it is rejected as today. If it rejects and the unit is unsure, the machine has no verdict: a decline, never a rejection. Three source files: \code{Reduce} (the first lines of \code{\verb~|k_iota3~} and one slow method beside it), \code{Infer} (one flag, two counters, the reading of the major premise that section 4.5 describes, about 100 lines moved in from the first candidate), \code{MachineSession} (one block of nine lines after the run and before the handling of eager reduction). Nothing in \code{Eval} or \code{Values}; no second run; no mode.}
\li{\strong{Variant B} = A, the flags that the first candidate sets in \code{Eval} (an over-application, a let variable with one type whose value is a lambda, the mark for a later declaration of the session), and the exact-let mode as an \em{accept-only second chance}: after the rejection of an unsure unit the unit runs again in the mode, where the K rule fires if it fires under \em{any} reading of the let variables that the type of the major premise goes through. An accept of that run is the unit's accept; a rejection, or any of the mode's own ends without a verdict, is a decline. The mode is never the source of a rejection, and the scope decides nothing: the scope stack, the marks, the two scope declines, the rule for an over-application and the collection of let variables behind a lambda body are no longer consulted. Their code is still in the patch of B, dead; it was left there so that the patch stays comparable with the reviewed one, and it has to go if B is taken.}
\li{\strong{A middle variant} was measured because the numbers asked for it: B's build with the second run switched off (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letsecond=off~}), that is A plus the flags of \code{Eval}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{13.3 Real input}

\p{Private builds of the current main tree (published build \code{d867a0766427}; the machine's sources are those of 22:44 UTC): base = the unpatched tree (jars \code{f62c8667964a}, machine jar \code{1eaaf3e3655c}, the published one), A (jars \code{8f64ecda212f}), B (see 13.6). The executable unless said otherwise.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The eight corpora, 8 workers, variant A}: every unit of the class is accepted \em{in the first run}. Unsure units: Std 1, cedar 1, con-leche 1, navier-stokes-euler 16, cslib 7, Mathlib 63; rejections of an unsure unit: 0. The machine's declines of this class: \strong{0}, the six over-application units of section 8.4 included. The declines that are left are eager reduction (decline events: con-leche 2, cslib 8, Mathlib 8).}
\li{\strong{One worker, unit traces, against the base}: 86 units change their outcome, each from declined to accepted (Std 1, cedar 1, con-leche 1, navier-stokes-euler 16, cslib 7, Mathlib 60): all 86 of section 1. The units the reference engine decides go from 1, 1, 2, 16, 11, 64 to 0, 0, 1, 0, 4, 4. With \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.kunion=off~} the build computes what the base computes on all eight corpora: every count key but the two new ones, every unit's steps and outcome.}
\li{\strong{The real rows of the black-box review}: the 75 units as small exports: accepted by the machine alone, all 75 (the published machine alone: 2, and 73 declines). The 1,309 structural mutants that have an official verdict (1,184 accept, 125 reject): a plain run gives the official verdict on every one; no unsure accept of an official reject. The machine alone decides 1,285 of them and declines 24 (published: 265 and 1,044). The same for the middle variant and for B.}
\li{\strong{The differential engine}, 8 workers, the eight corpora, variant A: 8 accepts, no declaration on which the machine and the reference engine differ. \strong{Accept runs}: 8 and 4 workers through both launchers, 32 runs, 32 accepts, no anomaly; the largest resident set 4,560 MiB.}
\li{\strong{The number of workers}: the 1,519 generated files with several declarations in a plain run and under 1, 4 and 8 workers: one verdict per file, variant A.}
}
}

