}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No wrong reject that the published plain run does not have}, in any variant, on any input (0 of 12,619; also 0 on the 67 rows of section 5 and the 56 rows of the review from the source). No kind F input accepted.}
\li{\strong{The price in agreement, variant A}: of the 6,206 official rejects a plain run accepts 1,516, where the published build accepts 939: \strong{577 more accepts of kind T}. Wrong rejects fall from 678 to 453. The machine alone still declines 2,859 inputs, where the published machine declines 4,911. A plain run gives the official verdict on 10,650 inputs (published 11,002): the union rule buys fewer wrong rejects and fewer declines with more accepts of statements that the official checker rejects.}
\li{\strong{The middle variant} has fewer wrong rejects than A (416) \em{and} fewer accepts of official rejects (1,486), at the price of more declines of the machine alone (3,221): the flags of \code{Eval} make more rejections "no verdict", among them 37 wrong rejects of A.}
\li{\strong{Variant B}: the same 416 wrong rejects as the middle variant; 2,342 accepts of official rejects (1,403 more than the published build); 1,683 declines of the machine alone. The second run converts 1,538 declines into verdicts of the machine, 682 of them official accepts and 856 accepts of official rejects.}
\li{\strong{The rows of this report and of the review from the source}, plain run, official / accept of an official reject / wrong reject: the 67 rows of section 5: published 54 / 8 / 5, A 47 / 19 / 1, middle 48 / 19 / 0, B 40 / 27 / 0. The 56 rows of the review: published 33 / 7 / 16, A 40 / 7 / 9, middle 40 / 7 / 9, B 37 / 10 / 9. The one wrong reject of A on the 67 rows is \code{mo2-overapp-declared-fires-ok} (finding MO2-F5: its flag sits in \code{Eval}); the nine on the 56 rows are the trigger gaps that the review names and that are out of scope here.}
\li{\strong{Where the published machine decided by itself and the candidate's machine declines}: variant A, 482 inputs of the three sets and the real grafts, \em{every one an official reject} that the published machine rejected: under the union rule the rejection of an unsure unit is never a verdict, so an official reject whose run made a K comparison of the class goes to the reference engine. No official accept is among them. (Another 230 are official accepts that the published machine rejected and A declines: wrong rejects turned into declines.) For the middle variant the same count is 704, and 15 generated files with several declarations that the published machine accepts by itself are declined: a later declaration is rejected in a session that the earlier, now accepted, unit did not end. For B: 337.}
\li{\strong{Rows of the suite that change their kind.} Under A the two strict rows that record finding E1-F6, \code{e1-sys-let-krule-major-applied-bad} and \code{e1-sib-krule-major-proj-applied-let-bad} (official reject, statement \code{True}), are accepted by the machine: it fires K under the type of the value. They are marked kind T in the patch. That is the closing of finding E1-F6 turned round: on 2026-11-24 it was closed by declining because the accept was not the official verdict; the union rule accepts it because it is a derivation. Under B two more rows change in the same way (\code{e1-sys-let-krule-major-variable-bad}, \code{e1-sib-krule-major-proj-let-bad}).}
\li{\strong{The order of declarations} that the black-box review found in the published build (the identity redex of finding MO2-F3 over a let variable: "inside, then after" accepted, "after, then inside" rejected, both official accepts) is the same under A and under B: it is a history of the first run in a class that is out of scope here.}
\li{\strong{The path and the schedule.} Under A there is one run, so a unit cannot be answered by one path under one schedule and by another under another. Whether a unit becomes \em{unsure} can still depend on the history of the session (the application of a let variable found in a tabling record is not applied again, and A has no mark for it; B has). That matters only for a unit the first run rejects: it is then rejected in one history and declined in another, and in a plain run the decline is the reference engine's verdict. Under B an accept is an accept on either path (first run or second run), and a second run can only replace a decline by an accept.}
}
}

