\tr{\td{Variant A}\td{0.903 (-0.0 \%)}\td{0.884 / 0.926 / 0.886 / 0.861 / 0.915 / 0.947}\td{6.8}\td{1,467 MiB}}
\tr{\td{Variant B}\td{0.914 (+1.1 \%)}\td{0.896 / 0.896 / 0.928 / 0.883 / 0.946 / 0.932}\td{6.9}\td{1,472 MiB}}
}

\p{\strong{Reading.} With six rounds in an undisturbed window variant A is at the base on Std, and B at +1.1 \%. The +4.4 \% of the first window does not repeat; neither do the +3.5 \% and +3.3 \% of the first candidate say anything about A, which is another build. What A adds to a first run is one test and one call per K comparison (about 4,000 of them on Std), and its ticks on Std are +0.011 \%. \strong{Variant A passes the guard on both launchers as far as it was measured}: every cell of the executable within 3 \% (the base's disturbed Init round set aside), every cell of the JVM launcher within 3 \% but Std in the disturbed window, and Std at -0.0 \% when measured again. \strong{Not measured}: the whole table of both launchers in undisturbed windows; those windows were queued behind four others and withdrawn. For variant B the cells cslib +6.6 \% and Mathlib +2.9 \% of the disturbed JVM window (rounds of the base on cslib 3.688 to 4.230 s) were not measured again.}

}

\subtree{
\title{13.7 For the integrator}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Variant A}: one patch, five files: \code{Infer}, \code{MachineSession}, \code{Reduce}, the module's README (one bullet), the manifest of the hostile suite (two rows become kind T). In \code{Reduce}: the comment and the slow method before \code{\verb~|k_iota3~} and its first lines (the old test of the flag); \code{\verb~|k_iota4~} is not touched. In \code{MachineSession.check0}: one block of nine lines between the eager restart and the test of the machine's own tick bound. Both are apart from what the union candidate for eager reduction changes, as far as its description goes. \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.kunion=off~} gives the rule of 2026-11-24 back, \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letroot=off~} takes the wider reading of the major premise out. Statistics \code{machine.let.kUnsureUnits} and \code{machine.let.kUnsureRejects}.}
\li{\strong{Variant B}: one patch, seven files (the five and \code{Eval}, \code{Values}); four rows become kind T. \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letsecond=off~} is the middle variant.}
\li{\strong{The rows}: the 67 rows of section 5, the 55 that the review from the source proposes and the 163 of the black-box review are not in these patches. Under the union rule their marks differ per variant (which official rejects are accepted), and the verdict tables of every variant on every one of them are handed over with the patches instead.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{13.8 What the union rule does not do, and a recommendation}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The trigger gaps that the published build has} stay: a redex, a let term or a projection of a constructor as the major premise, the binder of a redex, a let variable reached through a delayed term, a far frame (findings MO2-F3, MO2-F6 and the review's F2, F5, F6, F9, F10, F11). They are the 453 wrong rejects that A still has on the regression corpus, all of them the published build's own. They need the major premise typed as the term it is and are another package.}
\li{\strong{An official reject of the class is never a verdict of the machine} under A (482 inputs of the regression sets): the reference engine rejects it. On real input that is a unit of the class in a file that does not check, which the corpora do not have.}
\li{\strong{The count of kind T rises}: 577 inputs of the regression corpus, 11 rows of section 5, two rows of the suite. Each is an accept of a statement for which the machine has a derivation; that the statements of the generated inputs are derivable is the argument of 7.1, not something this package checked input by input, beyond the fact that no kind F input is accepted.}
\li{\strong{Recommendation.} Variant A does what was asked: on real input every unit of the class is a verdict of the machine in the first run, with no second run, no mode and no new state in the values; it has no wrong reject that the published build does not have. If the 37 wrong rejects that only the flags of \code{Eval} turn into declines are wanted, the middle variant is A plus those flags; it costs more declines of the machine alone and, on 15 generated files, declines where the published machine accepted. Variant B buys 682 official accepts for the machine alone on hostile input with 856 more accepts of official rejects and about 1,400 lines of the first candidate, half of them dead; this package does not recommend it.}
}
}
}
